passage('The path of a borrowed word',`A word for a woven storage basket appears in several languages spoken along an old trading route. Its similar form has often been cited as proof that one community invented the basket and taught all the others to make it. A linguist examining the evidence proposed a narrower conclusion.

Words can travel with objects, but they can also replace older names for objects already familiar to speakers. Traders may favor a common term because it simplifies exchange. In addition, the earliest written occurrence of a word is only the earliest one that happens to survive. It need not mark the first time the word was spoken or the object produced.

The linguist compared historical spellings, local basket-making techniques, and archaeological fragments. The vocabulary supported contact among communities, while variations in construction suggested more than one local tradition. No single source established a clear direction of invention and teaching.

The study did not make the shared word unimportant. It changed what the word could responsibly demonstrate. As evidence of exchange, it was strong when combined with other records. As a patent assigning the invention to one community, it exceeded the precision of the surviving evidence. The distinction allowed contact and local creativity to be recognized together.`,[
 ['main','What is the central conclusion?',['A shared word supports contact more securely than a single origin of invention','Kemiripan kata lebih kuat untuk bukti kontak daripada hak cipta asal.'],[['All basket-making methods were identical','Teknik menunjukkan beberapa tradisi.'],['The first surviving text always marks invention','Bacaan menolak anggapan ini.'],['Borrowed words provide no historical evidence','Kata tetap penting sebagai bukti pertukaran.']],'As evidence of exchange… As a patent… exceeded the precision','Kontras membatasi klaim yang dapat didukung.'],
 ['detail','Why might traders adopt a common term?',['To make exchange easier','Istilah bersama menyederhanakan perdagangan.'],[['To ensure older baskets stop existing','Nama dapat berganti meski objek sudah dikenal.'],['To prove every trader speaks only one language','Tidak ada klaim monolingual.'],['To eliminate all local techniques','Teknik lokal tetap beragam.']],'because it simplifies exchange','Alasan langsung dalam paragraf kedua.'],
 ['inference','What can be inferred about the earliest written occurrence?',['Earlier spoken uses may have left no surviving record','Bukti tertulis awal bukan awal absolut.'],[['It must have been written by the inventor','Tidak ada identifikasi inventor.'],['It proves the basket was unknown elsewhere','Ketiadaan catatan tidak membuktikan tidak dikenal.'],['It is necessarily older than every archaeological fragment','Tidak diberikan perbandingan usia itu.']],'only the earliest one that happens to survive','Survival dokumen membatasi kronologi yang diketahui.'],
 ['vocabulary','“Narrower” most nearly means',['more limited in scope','Kesimpulan dibatasi pada kontak alih-alih penemu tunggal.'],[['less physically wide','Bukan ukuran benda.'],['more difficult to pronounce','Bukan pengucapan.'],['longer in written form','Bukan panjang tulisan.']],'proposed a narrower conclusion','Konteks adalah cakupan klaim sejarah.'],
 ['purpose','Why compare the word to a “patent”?',['To criticize using linguistic similarity to assign exclusive credit for invention','Metafora menunjukkan klaim kepemilikan yang terlalu kuat.'],[['To announce a legal case against the communities','Tidak ada perkara hukum aktual.'],['To describe the process of applying for modern trademarks','Tidak ada prosedur merek dagang.'],['To claim that every basket was sold under one license','Tidak membahas lisensi perdagangan.']],'assigning the invention to one community','Patent dipakai secara kiasan untuk atribusi eksklusif.']
],'final');
